#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wiki-plain — the READER'S DIGEST layer: one plain-language twin per page.

WHAT THIS IS. Every page in `wiki/` is written for a reader who already knows
the corpus: typed edges, register tables, falsifier lists, provenance
disclosures, `knowledge: earned` and the rest. That is the correct register for
the technical wiki and it is unreadable to somebody who walked in off the
street. So each page may carry a twin under `plain/` — the same finding, at the
same altitude, told to a person who has never read anything else here.

    wiki/mind/synthesis/fayette-return.md   the entry
    plain/mind/synthesis/fayette-return.md  the same entry, in plain English

The portal serves the pair as two MODES behind one switch. `plain/` is not a
second wiki and its files are not pages: no typed edges, no `synthesizes:`, no
Gaps section, no page in the index, nothing to cite. A `plain/` file is a
RENDERING of a page and has no standing of its own.

WHY `plain/` AND NOT `digest/`. `bin/wiki-digest` and `DIGEST.md` already exist
and mean something completely different — the generated ledger of recent
activity. A second thing called "digest" in the same repository is a thing a
future session will conflate with the first, and `bin/wiki-digest` WRITES
files. The reader-facing name is "Reader's Digest" because that is what the
operator asked for; the path is `plain/` so nothing here can be mistaken for
the ledger.

WHY IT IS OUTSIDE `wiki/`. `bin/wiki-lint` globs `wiki/**/*.md` and every page
it finds is a page: it wants frontmatter, typed edges, an index entry, and it
counts toward the master index. A twin living in `wiki/` would double the page
count, orphan-warn forever, and — worst — become citable, so a later synthesis
could reason from a simplification instead of from the finding. Outside `wiki/`
it cannot.

---- THE ONE RULE THAT MATTERS -----------------------------------------------

**A translation records the version it was made from, and goes stale when that
version moves.** `source_modified:` in a `plain/` file is the `date_modified:`
of the page it was written against. When the page is revised, the twin is
stale, `check` says so, and it stays said until somebody re-reads the page and
re-translates it.

This is the same discipline as `bin/wiki-climb check` and it exists for the
same reason. A simplification that silently lags its source is worse than no
simplification: it is a confident, readable, WRONG account of what the wiki
says, served to exactly the reader least equipped to catch it. Never clear a
stale twin by bumping `source_modified:` — re-read the page, decide whether the
plain version survives the change, rewrite what moved.

---- THE MORATORIUM IS ENFORCED HERE, NOT REMEMBERED -------------------------

`CLAUDE.md` carries a standing operator directive: no new narrative about Annie
enters this repository. A plain-language retelling of an Annie page is new
narrative about Annie — it adds no facts, but it is new prose about a living
person in a situation the operator has called dangerous, written to be read by
people who could not read the original. That is squarely inside what the
directive stops.

So it is not left to a session to remember. It is TWO mechanical rules, and the
second is the one that actually guarantees the outcome:

  1. A page SUBSTANTIALLY about her gets no twin at all. `check` fails if one
     exists; `new` refuses; `next` never proposes it. "Substantially" is
     mechanical: named anywhere in the frontmatter (title, aliases, tags,
     sources, `synthesizes:`, a typed-edge target or claim), or more than
     `INCIDENTAL` mentions in the body.
  2. **No `plain/` file may mention her at all, ever, whatever its source.**
     `check` fails on any twin that does. This is the guarantee: rule 1 draws
     a line somebody could argue about, and rule 2 means no new prose about
     her can enter this layer regardless of where the line fell.

Rule 1 is not merely rule 2 applied earlier. A page can be *about* her while
mentioning her a handful of times, and translating one by quietly eliding her
would produce a twin that passes rule 2 and MISREPRESENTS THE PAGE — a reader
told a version of a finding with its subject removed has been misled more
efficiently than by any error. So: a page with an incidental mention is
eligible, and the twin simply does not carry that sentence. If leaving it out
would change what the page says, the page is not translatable — decline it, and
say so where the next session will read it. That judgment belongs to a reader;
the tool only holds the floor under it.

Lifting any of this is the operator's call and nobody else's — it is one
regex and one integer, `MORATORIUM` and `INCIDENTAL`, directly below.

Missing twins are NOT errors. Coverage is standing campaign work, worked
top-down like every other queue in this repository, and a page with no plain
version simply serves the technical one with a note saying so. Only a BROKEN
twin — stale, orphaned, or forbidden — is a gate failure.

    bin/wiki-plain status          # coverage, by domain, and what is stale
    bin/wiki-plain check           # the gate: 0 = clean, 1 = broken twin
    bin/wiki-plain next [n]        # the n densest pages with no twin yet
    bin/wiki-plain new <slug>      # scaffold a twin, frontmatter filled in
    bin/wiki-plain task [slug]     # one page's whole writing task, for an agent
    bin/wiki-plain audit [slug]    # the anti-slop referee: is this twin any good?
"""
import argparse
import os
import re
import sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
WIKI = os.path.join(ROOT, "wiki")
PLAIN = os.path.join(ROOT, "plain")

# The standing directive in CLAUDE.md, as code. See the two rules in the module
# docstring. Only the operator lifts either of these.
# Leading word boundary only, and no trailing one. `\bannie\b` looks tighter and
# is wrong: it misses `annie_metadata_24h.csv`, the filename this material is
# cited by throughout, because `_` is a word character and kills the boundary.
# read-receipt-forensics carries that filename twice and read as eligible until
# this was fixed. The surname rides along for the same reason — the guard should
# not depend on which of the two a given page happens to use.
MORATORIUM = re.compile(r"\b(annie|ulmer)", re.I)

# How many body mentions a page may carry and still count as incidental rather
# than as being about her. Two: a page that names somebody three times is
# talking about them. The 2026-08-28 distribution over 518 pages — 277 at zero,
# 24 at one, 32 at two, then a long tail — puts the natural break here.
INCIDENTAL = 2

# Archived artifacts are pinned oversized things nobody updates; they are exempt
# from budgets in wiki-lint and they are exempt from translation here too.
SKIP_DIRS = {".obsidian", "archive"}


def read(path):
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        return fh.read()


def frontmatter(text):
    """The shapes these files use — flat `key: value` only. Twins have no lists."""
    if not text.startswith("---"):
        return {}
    end = text.find("\n---", 3)
    if end == -1:
        return {}
    meta = {}
    for line in text[4:end].split("\n"):
        m = re.match(r"^([A-Za-z0-9_]+):\s*(.*)$", line)
        if m and m.group(2).strip():
            meta[m.group(1)] = m.group(2).strip().strip("\"'")
    return meta


def body_of(text):
    """Everything after the frontmatter block."""
    if not text.startswith("---"):
        return text
    end = text.find("\n---", 3)
    if end == -1:
        return text
    nl = text.find("\n", end + 1)
    return text[nl + 1:] if nl != -1 else ""


def words(text):
    return len(body_of(text).split())


def walk(root):
    """Slugs (`domain/path/name`, no extension) under a tree, skipping SKIP_DIRS."""
    out = []
    if not os.path.isdir(root):
        return out
    for dirpath, dirs, files in os.walk(root):
        dirs[:] = [d for d in dirs if d not in SKIP_DIRS and not d.startswith(".")]
        for f in sorted(files):
            if not f.endswith(".md"):
                continue
            rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dirpath, f), root)
            out.append(rel.replace(os.sep, "/")[:-3])
    return sorted(out)


class Page:
    """One wiki page and whatever the Reader's Digest layer has to say about it."""

    def __init__(self, slug):
        self.slug = slug
        self.src_path = os.path.join(WIKI, *slug.split("/")) + ".md"
        self.twin_path = os.path.join(PLAIN, *slug.split("/")) + ".md"
        src = read(self.src_path)
        self.meta = frontmatter(src)
        self.domain = slug.split("/")[0]
        self.page_type = self.meta.get("page_type", "")
        self.modified = self.meta.get("date_modified", "")
        self.words = words(src)

        # MORATORIUM RULE 1, judged on the source text rather than on the slug:
        # a page can be titled for somebody else entirely and still be carrying
        # her narrative. Frontmatter counts at any density — a name in
        # `synthesizes:`, in a typed-edge claim or in `sources:` means the page
        # is built on that material however sparingly the body says so.
        head = src[: src.find("\n---", 3)] if src.startswith("---") else ""
        self.excluded = bool(MORATORIUM.search(head)) or (
            len(MORATORIUM.findall(body_of(src))) > INCIDENTAL
        )

        self.twin = os.path.exists(self.twin_path)
        twin_src = read(self.twin_path) if self.twin else ""
        self.twin_meta = frontmatter(twin_src) if self.twin else {}
        self.twin_words = words(twin_src) if self.twin else 0
        # MORATORIUM RULE 2 — the guarantee. Nothing in this layer names her.
        self.twin_names_her = bool(MORATORIUM.search(twin_src)) if self.twin else False

    @property
    def stale(self):
        """The page moved after the twin was written against it."""
        if not self.twin:
            return False
        against = self.twin_meta.get("source_modified", "")
        return bool(self.modified) and against != self.modified

    @property
    def ratio(self):
        return (self.twin_words / self.words) if self.words and self.twin else 0.0


def load():
    return [Page(s) for s in walk(WIKI)]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def cmd_check(_args):
    """The gate. Broken twins only — a missing twin is work, not a defect."""
    pages = load()
    by_slug = {p.slug: p for p in pages}
    errors = []

    for p in pages:
        # Rule 2 first: it is the guarantee, and it holds even where rule 1
        # decided the source page was fine to work from.
        if p.twin_names_her:
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: plain/{p.slug}.md names a person under the standing "
                f"moratorium in CLAUDE.md. No file in this layer may, whatever "
                f"its source page says. Remove the passage — and if removing it "
                f"changes what the page means, delete the twin instead and "
                f"record the page as one that cannot be translated."
            )
        if p.twin and p.excluded:
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: has a plain/ twin but the page is under the standing "
                f"moratorium in CLAUDE.md. Delete plain/{p.slug}.md — a plain-"
                f"language retelling is new narrative, and only the operator "
                f"lifts this."
            )
        if p.stale:
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: twin is STALE — written against "
                f"{p.twin_meta.get('source_modified') or '(nothing)'}, page is now "
                f"{p.modified}. Re-read the page and re-translate what moved. "
                f"Never just bump source_modified:."
            )
        if p.twin and p.twin_meta.get("plain_of") != f"wiki/{p.slug}":
            errors.append(
                f"{p.slug}: twin's plain_of: is "
                f"'{p.twin_meta.get('plain_of', '(missing)')}', expected "
                f"'wiki/{p.slug}'."
            )

    # A twin with no page above it: the page was renamed or deleted and the
    # translation was left behind pointing at nothing.
    for slug in walk(PLAIN):
        if slug not in by_slug:
            errors.append(
                f"{slug}: plain/{slug}.md is an ORPHAN — no wiki/{slug}.md above "
                f"it. The page was renamed or removed; move or delete the twin."
            )

    for e in errors:
        print(f"  ERROR  {e}")
    covered = sum(1 for p in pages if p.twin)
    eligible = sum(1 for p in pages if not p.excluded)
    print(
        f"\nwiki-plain: {len(errors)} error(s) · {covered}/{eligible} eligible "
        f"pages have a Reader's Digest twin"
    )
    return 1 if errors else 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUDIT — the anti-slop referee
#
# `check` answers "is this twin BROKEN?" — stale, orphaned, forbidden. `audit`
# answers a different and harder question: "is this twin any GOOD?"
#
# It exists because the twins are the one layer of this repository a weak or
# careless writer can degrade without leaving a trace. A wrong synthesis
# contradicts a page somebody can go read. A slop twin reads fine, cites
# nothing, and is served to the reader least equipped to notice — and the
# writer, asked whether it is slop, will say no. So nothing here asks anyone's
# opinion. Every rule below is arithmetic over two files.
#
# The load-bearing one is FABRICATED NUMBERS. A twin may only contain numbers
# that appear in the page it translates. Simplifying the language is the job;
# inventing a quantity is manufacturing evidence, and it is the single most
# damaging thing a translator can do here, because a number reads as proof.
# Single digits are exempt (ordinary prose counts things: "two origins"); every
# multi-digit number, year and percentage is checked against the source.

# Numbers, dates and quantities. Commas stripped so "2,000" matches "2000".
NUMBER = re.compile(r"\d[\d,]*(?:\.\d+)?%?")

# A page often spells a quantity out ("thirty-five years", "Twenty-five works")
# where the twin quite properly writes the digits. That is a rendering choice,
# not an invented number, so the source's spelled forms are resolved to digits
# before anything is called fabricated. Only what a page plausibly spells out.
UNITS = {
    "one": 1, "two": 2, "three": 3, "four": 4, "five": 5, "six": 6, "seven": 7,
    "eight": 8, "nine": 9, "ten": 10, "eleven": 11, "twelve": 12,
    "thirteen": 13, "fourteen": 14, "fifteen": 15, "sixteen": 16,
    "seventeen": 17, "eighteen": 18, "nineteen": 19,
}
TENS = {
    "twenty": 20, "thirty": 30, "forty": 40, "fifty": 50,
    "sixty": 60, "seventy": 70, "eighty": 80, "ninety": 90,
}
SPELLED = re.compile(
    r"\b(" + "|".join(list(TENS) + list(UNITS)) + r")(?:[-\s](" + "|".join(UNITS) + r"))?\b",
    re.I,
)


def spelled_numbers(text):
    """Digit strings for every number the text spells out in words."""
    out = set()
    for m in SPELLED.finditer(text):
        head, tail = m.group(1).lower(), (m.group(2) or "").lower()
        if head in TENS:
            out.add(str(TENS[head] + UNITS.get(tail, 0)))
        else:
            out.add(str(UNITS[head]))
    return out

# Machinery that belongs to the technical edition and must not survive into the
# plain one. A reader who has never been here does not need a `raw/` path.
APPARATUS = [
    (re.compile(r"\[\[[^\]]+\]\]"), "a [[wiki/...]] link"),
    (re.compile(r"\braw/[a-z0-9_./-]+", re.I), "a raw/ source path"),
    (re.compile(r"^\s*(synthesizes|connections|page_type|knowledge|date_range_\w+|tags|aliases):", re.M), "a frontmatter key that belongs to the page, not the twin"),
    (re.compile(r"\b(typed edge|instantiates|component-of|evidenced-by|constitution pass|provenance disclosure)\b", re.I), "wiki-maintenance vocabulary"),
    (re.compile(r"\bknowledge:\s*(earned|derived|mixed)\b", re.I), "a knowledge: value"),
]

# The house style of a model writing to fill space. Not a taste judgement —
# every one of these is a phrase that adds no claim, and a twin is 100% claims.
SLOP = [
    r"\bit'?s (important|worth) (to note|noting|remembering)\b",
    r"\bit should be noted\b",
    r"\bdelve[sd]? into\b",
    r"\ba (rich )?tapestry\b",
    r"\bnavigat(e|ing) the (complexit|landscape|nuance)",
    r"\bmultifacet(ed)?\b",
    r"\bplays? a (crucial|key|vital|significant) role\b",
    r"\bin (today'?s|the modern) world\b",
    r"\bin conclusion\b",
    r"\bthis (article|document|page) (will )?(explore|examine|discuss)",
    r"\bfurthermore,",
    r"\bmoreover,",
    r"\bit is (clear|evident) that\b",
    r"\ba testament to\b",
    r"\bunderscore[sd]? the importance\b",
    r"\bsheds? light on\b",
    r"\bat the end of the day\b",
    r"\bwhen it comes to\b",
]

# The page admits what it does not know, and so must the twin.
SOURCE_HONESTY = re.compile(r"^#{2,3}\s+.*\b(gaps?|falsifiers?|what would|open questions|unresolved)\b", re.I | re.M)
TWIN_HONESTY = re.compile(r"^#{2,3}\s+.*(prove this wrong|don'?t know|not sure|unknown|gaps?|falsif|gets? this wrong|missing)\b", re.I | re.M)

POINTER = re.compile(r"(full|technical|complete)\s+(entry|page|version)", re.I)

# `new` and `task` leave angle-bracket prompts in the scaffold. A twin still
# carrying one was abandoned mid-write, and the tree should say so rather than
# publish a template.
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(r"<[A-Z][^>\n]{12,}>")


def sentences(text):
    return [s for s in re.split(r"[.!?]+(?:\s|$)", text) if s.strip()]


def syllables(word):
    """Good enough for a grade level; this is a smoke alarm, not a linguist."""
    word = re.sub(r"[^a-z]", "", word.lower())
    if not word:
        return 0
    groups = re.findall(r"[aeiouy]+", word)
    n = len(groups)
    if word.endswith("e") and n > 1 and not word.endswith(("le", "ee")):
        n -= 1
    return max(1, n)


def grade_level(text):
    """Flesch-Kincaid. The plain edition is for people who have not read the wiki."""
    prose = re.sub(r"^\s*\|.*$", "", text, flags=re.M)          # tables are not prose
    prose = re.sub(r"[*_`#>\[\]]", " ", prose)
    words = re.findall(r"[A-Za-z][A-Za-z'-]*", prose)
    sents = sentences(prose)
    if not words or not sents:
        return 0.0
    syl = sum(syllables(w) for w in words)
    return 0.39 * (len(words) / len(sents)) + 11.8 * (syl / len(words)) - 15.59


def audit_page(p):
    """(errors, warnings) for one translated page. Arithmetic, never opinion."""
    errors, warnings = [], []
    src = read(p.src_path)
    twin = read(p.twin_path)
    src_body, twin_body = body_of(src), body_of(twin)

    # -- 1. fabricated numbers ------------------------------------------------
    norm = lambda m: m.group(0).replace(",", "").rstrip(".")
    src_nums = {norm(m) for m in NUMBER.finditer(src_body)}
    src_nums |= spelled_numbers(src_body)
    # A four-digit year licenses its own shorthand: a page writing "2011-2012"
    # and a twin writing "2011-12" are the same date, not a new one.
    src_nums |= {n[2:] for n in list(src_nums) if len(n) == 4 and n.isdigit() and n.startswith(("18", "19", "20"))}
    invented = sorted(
        {
            norm(m)
            for m in NUMBER.finditer(twin_body)
            if len(norm(m).rstrip("%").replace(".", "")) > 1 and norm(m) not in src_nums
        },
        key=lambda v: (len(v), v),
    )
    if invented:
        errors.append(
            "FABRICATED NUMBERS — these appear in the twin but nowhere in the page: "
            + ", ".join(invented[:12])
            + ". Every quantity must be traceable to the page. Delete it or use the "
            "page's own figure; do not round, convert, total or estimate."
        )

    # -- 2. evidence quietly dropped -----------------------------------------
    kept = len({n for n in src_nums if n in {norm(m) for m in NUMBER.finditer(twin_body)}})
    big_src = {n for n in src_nums if len(n.rstrip("%").replace(".", "")) > 1}
    if big_src:
        keep_rate = len({n for n in big_src if n in twin_body.replace(",", "")}) / len(big_src)
        if keep_rate < 0.25:
            warnings.append(
                f"only {keep_rate * 100:.0f}% of the page's figures survive into the twin "
                "— check you are simplifying the language and not the evidence"
            )

    # -- 3. apparatus that should have been stripped --------------------------
    for pattern, what in APPARATUS:
        m = pattern.search(twin)
        if m:
            errors.append(
                f"APPARATUS LEAKED — {what}: {m.group(0)[:60]!r}. That is machinery for "
                "maintaining the wiki, not part of the finding. Remove it."
            )

    # -- 4. slop ---------------------------------------------------------------
    for pattern in SLOP:
        m = re.search(pattern, twin_body, re.I)
        if m:
            errors.append(
                f"FILLER PHRASE — {m.group(0)!r}. It carries no claim. Cut the sentence "
                "or replace it with what actually happened."
            )

    # -- 5. the honest half ----------------------------------------------------
    if SOURCE_HONESTY.search(src_body) and not TWIN_HONESTY.search(twin_body):
        errors.append(
            "MISSING THE HONEST HALF — the page states its gaps or what would prove it "
            "wrong, and the twin does not. A reader shown only the confident half is "
            "being sold something. Add those sections in plain words."
        )

    # -- 6. length band --------------------------------------------------------
    if p.words and p.twin_words:
        ratio = p.twin_words / p.words
        if ratio < 0.18:
            errors.append(
                f"TOO SHORT — {p.twin_words} words against the page's {p.words} "
                f"({ratio * 100:.0f}%). That is a summary, not a translation. A twin "
                "carries the same finding at the same altitude."
            )
        elif ratio > 0.95:
            warnings.append(
                f"barely shorter than the page ({ratio * 100:.0f}%) — check the apparatus "
                "was actually dropped"
            )

    # -- 7. reading level ------------------------------------------------------
    grade = grade_level(twin_body)
    if grade > 12.5:
        errors.append(
            f"TOO HARD TO READ — Flesch-Kincaid grade {grade:.1f}, target 12 or below. "
            "Shorten the sentences and swap the long words. Keep every fact."
        )

    # -- 8. an abandoned scaffold ---------------------------------------------
    m = PLACEHOLDER.search(twin_body)
    if m:
        errors.append(
            f"UNFILLED TEMPLATE — the scaffold prompt {m.group(0)[:50]!r} is still here. "
            "Either finish the twin or delete the file; a half-written one blocks the gate "
            "on purpose."
        )

    # -- 9. the way back -------------------------------------------------------
    if not POINTER.search(twin_body):
        errors.append(
            "NO POINTER BACK — close with the standard line telling the reader the full "
            "entry exists and carries the sources."
        )

    return errors, warnings


def cmd_audit(args):
    pages = [p for p in load() if p.twin]
    if args.slug:
        want = args.slug.removeprefix("wiki/").removesuffix(".md")
        pages = [p for p in pages if p.slug == want]
        if not pages:
            print(f"No twin at plain/{want}.md", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    failed = 0
    for p in pages:
        errors, warnings = audit_page(p)
        grade = grade_level(body_of(read(p.twin_path)))
        if errors or warnings or args.slug:
            print(f"\n{p.slug}  ({p.words} -> {p.twin_words} words, grade {grade:.1f})")
        for e in errors:
            print(f"  FAIL  {e}")
        for w in warnings:
            print(f"  warn  {w}")
        if errors:
            failed += 1
        elif args.slug:
            print("  PASS  nothing mechanical to object to.")

    print(f"\nwiki-plain audit: {len(pages)} twin(s), {failed} failing")
    return 1 if failed else 0


def cmd_status(args):
    pages = load()
    eligible = [p for p in pages if not p.excluded]
    covered = [p for p in pages if p.twin]
    stale = [p for p in pages if p.stale]

    print("READER'S DIGEST COVERAGE\n")
    print(f"  pages in wiki/          {len(pages)}")
    print(f"  eligible                {len(eligible)}")
    print(f"  withheld (moratorium)   {len(pages) - len(eligible)}")
    print(f"  translated              {len(covered)}")
    print(f"  stale                   {len(stale)}")
    if eligible:
        print(f"  coverage                {100 * len(covered) / len(eligible):.1f}%")

    print("\n  BY DOMAIN")
    for d in sorted({p.domain for p in pages}):
        rows = [p for p in pages if p.domain == d]
        el = [p for p in rows if not p.excluded]
        cv = [p for p in rows if p.twin]
        print(f"    {d:<12} {len(cv):>3}/{len(el):<4} translated   ({len(rows)} pages)")

    if covered:
        print("\n  TRANSLATED")
        for p in sorted(covered, key=lambda p: p.slug):
            flag = "  ** STALE **" if p.stale else ""
            print(
                f"    {p.slug:<48} {p.words:>6} -> {p.twin_words:<6} "
                f"({p.ratio * 100:.0f}%){flag}"
            )

    if stale:
        print("\n  STALE — re-read the page, then re-translate what moved:")
        for p in stale:
            print(
                f"    {p.slug}  (twin written against "
                f"{p.twin_meta.get('source_modified') or '(nothing)'}, "
                f"page now {p.modified})"
            )
    return 0


def cmd_next(args):
    """Densest eligible pages with no twin — the queue, worked top-down."""
    todo = [p for p in load() if not p.twin and not p.excluded]
    todo.sort(key=lambda p: -p.words)
    if args.synthesis:
        todo = [p for p in todo if p.page_type == "synthesis"]
    if not todo:
        print("Nothing eligible is untranslated.")
        return 0
    print(f"NEXT {min(args.n, len(todo))} — densest first, {len(todo)} outstanding\n")
    for p in todo[: args.n]:
        kind = p.page_type or "-"
        print(f"  {p.words:>6}w  {kind:<12} {p.slug}")
    print("\n  bin/wiki-plain new <slug>   to scaffold one")
    return 0


SCAFFOLD = """---
plain_of: wiki/{slug}
title: "{title}"
reading_level: general
date_modified: {today}
source_modified: {source_modified}
---

# {title}

**The short version.** <One or two sentences. What would you tell somebody in
an elevator? No jargon, no citations, no hedging — the finding itself.>

## <A plain heading, phrased as what a reader would ask>

<Write it the way you would explain it to a smart person who has never read
this wiki. Keep every fact and every number. Drop the apparatus: typed edges,
register tables, provenance disclosures and `knowledge:` values are machinery
for maintaining the wiki, not part of the finding.>

## What would prove this wrong

<The technical page's falsifiers, in plain words. Keep them. A reader who is
only shown the confident half is being sold something.>

## What we still don't know

<The gaps, in plain words. Same reason.>
"""


TASK_RULES = """\
RULES — the tool enforces these; you cannot talk your way past them.

  1. Write ONE file: plain/{slug}.md. Touch nothing else. Never edit wiki/.
  2. Every number, date and quantity in what you write MUST appear in the page
     above. Do not round, convert, total, estimate or add a denominator. If you
     cannot point at it in the page, delete it.
  3. Keep the falsifiers and the gaps. Put them under plain headings a stranger
     would use: "What would prove this wrong" and "What we still don't know".
     A reader shown only the confident half is being sold something.
  4. Drop the apparatus: [[wiki/...]] links, raw/ paths, typed edges,
     synthesizes:, knowledge:, register tables, provenance notes.
  5. Explain jargon, do not delete the concept. "Ti-dominance with no
     Fe-mediated grading function" becomes "his sense of his own worth runs on
     an on/off switch with nothing in between".
  6. Short sentences. Target US grade 12 or below. Keep every fact.
  7. Aim for 30-60% of the page's length. Shorter than 18% is a summary, and a
     summary is not what this is.
  8. Close with a line telling the reader the full entry exists and carries the
     sources.
  9. Do not invent a finding, soften a conclusion, or add an opinion the page
     does not make. You are re-wording an argument, not having one.

THEN, and this is not optional:

    bin/wiki-plain audit {slug}

Fix every FAIL it prints and run it again. When it prints PASS, run
`bin/wiki-check` and commit. If you cannot get it to PASS in three tries,
delete plain/{slug}.md, say why in one line, and move to the next page.
"""


def cmd_task(args):
    """Everything a writer needs for one page, in one blob.

    Written for an agent that will not remember the rules from last time and
    will not go and read a spec. The rules ride along with every task, and the
    whole page is printed rather than named — a twin written from the first
    three paragraphs is a summary, and that is the most common way this goes
    wrong.
    """
    if args.slug:
        slug = args.slug.removeprefix("wiki/").removesuffix(".md")
        pages = [p for p in load() if p.slug == slug]
        if not pages:
            print(f"No such page: wiki/{slug}.md", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        p = pages[0]
        if p.excluded:
            print(
                f"REFUSED: wiki/{slug}.md is withheld under the standing "
                f"moratorium in CLAUDE.md. Pick another page.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 2
    else:
        todo = [p for p in load() if not p.twin and not p.excluded]
        if args.synthesis:
            todo = [p for p in todo if p.page_type == "synthesis"]
        if not todo:
            print("NOTHING TO DO — every eligible page has a twin.")
            return 0
        todo.sort(key=lambda p: -p.words)
        p = todo[0]

    if not p.twin:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p.twin_path), exist_ok=True)
        import datetime

        with open(p.twin_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(
                SCAFFOLD.format(
                    slug=p.slug,
                    title=p.meta.get("title", p.slug.split("/")[-1]).strip("\"'"),
                    today=datetime.date.today().isoformat(),
                    source_modified=p.modified,
                )
            )
        scaffolded = "scaffolded just now"
    else:
        scaffolded = "already exists — you are revising it"

    bar = "=" * 72
    print(f"{bar}\nTASK: write the plain-English twin of wiki/{p.slug}.md")
    print(f"WRITE TO: plain/{p.slug}.md   ({scaffolded})")
    print(f"LENGTH: the page is {p.words} words. Aim for {int(p.words * 0.3)}-{int(p.words * 0.6)}.")
    print(bar)
    print(TASK_RULES.format(slug=p.slug))
    print(bar)
    print(f"THE PAGE — read all of it before you write a word.\n{bar}\n")
    print(read(p.src_path))
    print(bar)
    return 0


def cmd_new(args):
    slug = args.slug.removeprefix("wiki/").removesuffix(".md")
    src = os.path.join(WIKI, *slug.split("/")) + ".md"
    if not os.path.exists(src):
        print(f"No such page: wiki/{slug}.md", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    page = Page(slug)
    if page.excluded:
        print(
            f"REFUSED: wiki/{slug}.md is under the standing moratorium in "
            f"CLAUDE.md.\nA plain-language retelling is new narrative about a "
            f"living person. Only the operator lifts this.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 2
    if page.twin:
        print(f"plain/{slug}.md already exists.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    import datetime

    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(page.twin_path), exist_ok=True)
    with open(page.twin_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(
            SCAFFOLD.format(
                slug=slug,
                title=page.meta.get("title", slug.split("/")[-1]).strip("\"'"),
                today=datetime.date.today().isoformat(),
                source_modified=page.modified,
            )
        )
    print(f"Wrote plain/{slug}.md  (source is {page.words} words)")
    print("Read the whole page before you translate it. Keep every number.")
    return 0


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split("\n")[0])
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd")

    sub.add_parser("check", help="gate: broken twins only")

    a = sub.add_parser("audit", help="anti-slop referee: is this twin any good?")
    a.add_argument("slug", nargs="?")
    sub.add_parser("status", help="coverage report")

    n = sub.add_parser("next", help="densest untranslated pages")
    n.add_argument("n", nargs="?", type=int, default=10)
    n.add_argument("--synthesis", action="store_true", help="synthesis pages only")

    w = sub.add_parser("new", help="scaffold a twin")
    w.add_argument("slug")

    t = sub.add_parser("task", help="one page's full writing task, rules and page included")
    t.add_argument("slug", nargs="?")
    t.add_argument("--synthesis", action="store_true", help="synthesis pages only")

    args = ap.parse_args()
    return {
        "check": cmd_check,
        "audit": cmd_audit,
        "status": cmd_status,
        "next": cmd_next,
        "new": cmd_new,
        "task": cmd_task,
        None: cmd_status,
    }[args.cmd](args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    # `status` is long and gets piped to `head` constantly. Without this that is
    # a BrokenPipeError traceback instead of output.
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except BrokenPipeError:
        os.dup2(os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY), sys.stdout.fileno())
        sys.exit(0)
